e, typically observed at a strain amplitude of ±0.50% and below. The value of the intrinsic-fatigue toughness ( W f ’ ) in Eq. ( 4 ) indicates fatigue-damage-tolerance capacity of materials, and  β , called the damage-transition exponent, indicates damage accumulation and defusing ability. A comparatively higher value of ( W f ’ ) in as-built alloys is acquired by higher ductility than in the aged alloy. However, a higher value of  β  in the aged alloy signifies a higher damage-defusing capability as compared to the as-built alloy. The higher damage accumulation in the as-built alloy leads to observed lower fatigue life at lower strain-amplitudes as compared to the aged alloy. Hence, both model parameters reflect a similar tendency and well correlate with the observed crossover of fatigue-life trend of aged and as-built alloys at around 0.50% strain amplitude. Further Ramberg–Osgood method 51 , which describes the relation between cyclic stress and strain response, was applied. The corresponding relation is described in Eq. ( 5 ). The values are plotted on the log-log scale as shown in Fig.  3e, f , and by linear-fitting cyclic-strength coefficient and cyclic-hardening exponent were estimated. The estimated cyclic-strength coefficient of the aged alloy is higher than that of the as-built alloy, which shows that the aged alloy has higher resistance to plastic deformation. This observation can be well correlated with the comparatively higher damage-defusing capability (Higher  β ), i.e., releasing more external applied energy as elastic energy and little accumulation as plastic deformation. On the other hand, a higher cyclic hardening exponent ( n’ ) shows a higher rate of cyclic-hardening as compared to the as-built alloy. The stress profile of the aged alloy shows almost no hardening up to 0.75%, which indicates comparatively lower plastic deformation than the as-built alloy, as reflected in the estimated  K’  parameter. The hardening for a smaller number of initial cycles at higher strain amplitude indicates a higher hardening rate, as reflected in the  n’  parameter. Cyclic hardening and softening compared to monotonic behavior are described and discussed in Supplementary Note  3  and Supplementary Fig.  S2 . Overall, the observed higher fatigue life of the aged alloy at lower strain amplitudes can be rationalized by lower plastic-strain accumulation, as reflected by the higher  β  value, whereas the reduced fatigue life at higher strain amplitudes may be related to its comparatively lower damage tolerance (lower  W f ’ ). These differences may be associated with distinct deformation mechanisms, such as wavy slip in the as-built alloy and planar slip in the aged alloy, as discussed in later sections. Fig. 3 Fatigue-life-prediction models of the Al 0.2 Co 1.5 CrFeNi 1.5 Ti 0.3  HEAs. a ,  b  Strain-fatigue life Coffin–Manson plot for the as-built and aged alloy, respectively,  c ,  d  plastic-strain energy-based model for the as-built alloy and aged alloy, respectively, and  e ,  f  the Ramberg–Osgood model for the as-built and aged alloy, respectively (numerical data for the plots are available in Supplementary Dataset  2 ). Table 1 The estimated fatigue parameters mentioned in Eqs. ( 1 – 5 ) Alloy σ f ’ b ϵ f ’ c W f ’ β K’ n' As-built 1426 −0.073 1.347 −0.720 1165 1.47 1418 0.103 Aged 2465 −0.112 0.189 −0.130 389 1.64 1922 0.155

To investigate the detailed mechanistic insights during deformation, we conducted in situ neutron-diffraction measurements subjected to fully reversed strain cycling at room temperature, using an MTS load frame with an attached thermocouple in the VULCAN Engineering material diffractometer at the Spallation Neutron Source (SNS), Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), as shown in Fig.  10a . Diffraction data are collected for intermittent fatigue cycles ( N ) ( N  = 1, 4, 8, 12, 30, 50, 100, 250, 500, and so on) to trace the different deformation stages of fatigue. Diffraction data are recorded at seven different points of the hysteresis loop, as mentioned in Fig.  10c . Among the multiple detector banks, two detector banks at ±90˚, simultaneously record a complete diffraction vector pattern from the grain parallel (loading direction) and perpendicular (transverse direction) to the applied load, respectively, as illustrated in Fig.  10a, b . The details of the experimental setup can be found in the reference (2019 instrument paper, MRS Bulletin 78 ). The time-of-flight diffraction was acquired with a count time of about 6 min to achieve sufficient scattering intensity for analyzing the diffraction profiles. The collected data were reduced and analyzed through the VULCAN data reduction and Interactive Visualization software (@VDRIVE) 79 . The diffraction profiles were analyzed for evaluating d-spacing, lattice constant, and phase fractions by applying a Pseudo–Voigt function for SPF, using the General Structure Analysis Software (GSAS) 80 . The hkl plane-specific lattice strains were determined by referencing the initial d-spacing measured from the pre-deformed value using the relation described below: 6 ε h k l = d h k l − d h k l 0 d h k l 0 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
